<Dungeons>
  <!--
    This file does two jobs.

    1. DENSITY TUNING for dungeons that already exist as C# classes. Give the entry the
       dungeon's exact display name and a <Density>. 1 = original, 0.5 = half as many
       enemies, 0 = none. Decorations are unchanged.

    2. DEFINING A WHOLE DUNGEON, with no C# needed. Add a <Key> and a <MapFile> and the
       entry becomes a real dungeon that can be opened by a gate key item or by a boss's
       create_gate death action. Both fields are required; with only one the entry falls
       back to being density-only and the server logs why.

    Data-driven dungeons cover the "load a map, play some music, kill the boss on a
    marker" shape. The procedurally generated dungeons (Dumir, Valdok's Forge, Bubra
    Barrens) build their maps in code and still need their own classes.

    NOTE ON DENSITY: it only works for dungeons that generate enemies in code. A
    data-driven dungeon takes its enemies from its map file, so <Density> has no
    effect on one - edit the map instead.
  -->

  <Dungeon name="Bubra Barrens">
    <Density>0.5</Density>
  </Dungeon>
  <Dungeon name="Valdok's Forge">
    <Density>0.5</Density>
  </Dungeon>
  <Dungeon name="Dumir">
    <Density>0.5</Density>
  </Dungeon>

  <!--
    ===================================================================
    TEMPLATE ONLY - DISABLED. The server does not load anything below.
    ===================================================================

    Everything from here to the closing marker sits inside this XML comment, so the
    parser never sees it and no dungeon is created. To turn it into a real dungeon:
    copy the block out above this comment, then change the values.

    Only name, Key, MapFile and Portal are required; everything else has a default.

  <Dungeon name="Dungeon 6">
    <Key>dungeon6</Key>                    Short name used by gate keys and create_gate
    <MapFile>dungeon6.mef</MapFile>        Lives in Server\...\World\Map\Files\
    <Portal>0xa86</Portal>                 Object id of the portal players click
    <Music>Dark_Recluse</Music>
    <MaxPlayers>20</MaxPlayers>
    <PortalTime>-1</PortalTime>            Seconds the portal stays open; -1 = until finished
    <TargetPlayers>2</TargetPlayers>       Player count the dungeon is balanced for
    <ScalePerPlayer>0.2</ScalePerPlayer>   Enemy health change per player above target
    <LimitSight />                         Presence of the tag turns it on
    <AllowPlayerTeleport />

    Objectives, completed in order. 'region' is a marker painted on the map;
    'index' picks which marker when the map has several of that type.
    <Boss id="0x1089" region="Tag1" />
    <Boss id="0x108a" region="Tag2" index="0" />
  </Dungeon>

    =================== end of disabled template ======================
  -->
</Dungeons>
